# Licensing

This repository contains code, original research writing and annotations, raw
language-model outputs, machine-generated review artifacts, and other
provenance material. A single blanket license would misdescribe the rights in
those different categories.

## 1. Project-authored software

Unless a file states otherwise, project-authored software in `scripts/`,
`tests/`, project HTML review tools, and supporting software/configuration code
is licensed under the **MIT License**.

See [`LICENSE-CODE`](LICENSE-CODE).

## 2. Project-authored research content

To the extent that Robert Leo Duffy III / Bo Chesterton holds the relevant
copyright or database rights, original project-authored research prose,
codebooks, human-created annotations, tabular results, figures, and
documentation are licensed under the **Creative Commons Attribution 4.0
International License (CC BY 4.0)**.

See [`LICENSE-CONTENT.md`](LICENSE-CONTENT.md).

## 3. Material excluded from the general license grants

The MIT and CC BY 4.0 grants above do **not** purport to grant rights the
project does not hold. In particular, they do not automatically apply to:

- raw or quoted language-model response text;
- machine-generated coding passes or machine-generated review manuscripts;
- third-party quotations, references, or incorporated material;
- provider trademarks, model names, or other third-party marks; or
- any other material identified as externally authored or separately licensed.

Such material is preserved in this repository for research transparency,
auditability, and reproducibility. Its inclusion does not imply that Robert Leo
Duffy III or Bo Chesterton owns it or grants additional reuse rights beyond
those available under applicable law, provider terms, or the material's own
license.

## 4. Mixed files

Some files combine project-authored annotations or commentary with model- or
third-party-generated text. The project licenses only the portions for which it
holds the relevant rights. No broader ownership claim is made over embedded
excluded material.

## Preferred attribution

For CC BY 4.0 material, a preferred attribution is:

> Robert Leo Duffy III (Bo Chesterton), *When Machines Say They Care: Study 1*,
> 2026, https://github.com/quumble/when-machines-say-they-care

Where practical, identify whether you used the technical manuscript, the
human-access paper, code, annotations, tables, or other specific material.

See `CITATION.cff` for machine-readable citation metadata.
